1
00:00:02,879 --> 00:00:08,160
The tidings of my high fortunes having had a heavy fall had got down to my native place

2
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:14,038
and its neighbourhood before I got there. I found the Blue Boar in possession of the intelligence,

3
00:00:14,038 --> 00:00:18,839
and I found that it made a great change in the Boar’s demeanour. Whereas the Boar

4
00:00:18,839 --> 00:00:37,033
had cultivated my good opinion with warm assiduity when I was coming into property,

5
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:37,116
the Boar was exceedingly cool on the subject now that I was going out of property.

6
00:00:37,116 --> 00:00:37,177
It was evening when I arrived, much fatigued by the journey I

7
00:00:37,177 --> 00:00:38,557
had so often made so easily. The Boar could not put me into my usual bedroom,

8
00:00:38,559 --> 00:00:43,957
which was engaged (probably by some one who had expectations), and could only assign me a

9
00:00:43,957 --> 00:00:49,917
very indifferent chamber among the pigeons and post-chaises up the yard. But I had as sound a

10
00:00:49,917 --> 00:00:54,796
sleep in that lodging as in the most superior accommodation the Boar could have given me,

11
00:00:54,796 --> 00:00:59,637
and the quality of my dreams was about the same as in the best bedroom.

12
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:05,680
Early in the morning, while my breakfast was getting ready, I strolled round by Satis House.

13
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:10,358
There were printed bills on the gate and on bits of carpet hanging out of the windows,

14
00:01:10,358 --> 00:01:16,879
announcing a sale by auction of the Household Furniture and Effects, next week. The House

15
00:01:16,879 --> 00:01:23,917
itself was to be sold as old building materials, and pulled down. Lot 1 was marked in whitewashed

16
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:29,078
knock-knee letters on the brew house; Lot 2 on that part of the main building which

17
00:01:29,078 --> 00:01:35,078
had been so long shut up. Other lots were marked off on other parts of the structure,

18
00:01:35,078 --> 00:01:40,436
and the ivy had been torn down to make room for the inscriptions, and much of it trailed low in

19
00:01:40,438 --> 00:01:47,559
the dust and was withered already. Stepping in for a moment at the open gate, and looking around me

20
00:01:47,559 --> 00:01:53,796
with the uncomfortable air of a stranger who had no business there, I saw the auctioneer’s clerk

21
00:01:53,796 --> 00:02:00,277
walking on the casks and telling them off for the information of a catalogue-compiler, pen in hand,

22
00:02:00,277 --> 00:02:06,635
who made a temporary desk of the wheeled chair I had so often pushed along to the tune of Old Clem.

23
00:02:06,637 --> 00:02:10,718
When I got back to my breakfast in the Boar’s coffee-room, I found Mr.

24
00:02:10,718 --> 00:02:16,917
Pumblechook conversing with the landlord. Mr. Pumblechook (not improved in appearance by

25
00:02:16,917 --> 00:02:23,438
his late nocturnal adventure) was waiting for me, and addressed me in the following terms:—

26
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:27,319
“Young man, I am sorry to see you brought low. But

27
00:02:27,319 --> 00:02:31,358
what else could be expected! what else could be expected!”

28
00:02:31,358 --> 00:02:35,277
As he extended his hand with a magnificently forgiving air,

29
00:02:35,277 --> 00:02:40,635
and as I was broken by illness and unfit to quarrel, I took it.

30
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:43,718
“William,” said Mr. Pumblechook to the waiter,

31
00:02:43,718 --> 00:02:49,758
“put a muffin on table. And has it come to this! Has it come to this!”

32
00:02:49,758 --> 00:02:54,559
I frowningly sat down to my breakfast. Mr. Pumblechook stood over me and poured

33
00:02:54,559 --> 00:02:58,277
out my tea—before I could touch the teapot—with

34
00:02:58,277 --> 00:03:03,199
the air of a benefactor who was resolved to be true to the last.

35
00:03:03,199 --> 00:03:10,000
“William,” said Mr. Pumblechook, mournfully, “put the salt on. In happier times,” addressing me, “I

36
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,559
think you took sugar? And did you take milk? You did. Sugar and milk. William, bring a watercress.”

37
00:03:17,637 --> 00:03:21,476
“Thank you,” said I, shortly, “but I don’t eat watercresses.”

38
00:03:21,476 --> 00:03:26,358
“You don’t eat ’em,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, sighing and nodding his head several times,

39
00:03:26,358 --> 00:03:30,756
as if he might have expected that, and as if abstinence from watercresses were consistent

40
00:03:30,758 --> 00:03:39,597
with my downfall. “True. The simple fruits of the earth. No. You needn’t bring any, William.”

41
00:03:39,597 --> 00:03:44,559
I went on with my breakfast, and Mr. Pumblechook continued to stand over me,

42
00:03:44,559 --> 00:03:49,957
staring fishily and breathing noisily, as he always did.

43
00:03:49,957 --> 00:03:53,319
“Little more than skin and bone!” mused Mr. Pumblechook,

44
00:03:53,319 --> 00:03:57,839
aloud. “And yet when he went from here (I may say with my blessing),

45
00:03:57,839 --> 00:04:04,879
and I spread afore him my humble store, like the Bee, he was as plump as a Peach!”

46
00:04:04,879 --> 00:04:09,438
This reminded me of the wonderful difference between the servile manner in which he had

47
00:04:09,438 --> 00:04:15,277
offered his hand in my new prosperity, saying, “May I?” and the ostentatious

48
00:04:15,277 --> 00:04:20,398
clemency with which he had just now exhibited the same fat five fingers.

49
00:04:20,398 --> 00:04:26,678
“Hah!” he went on, handing me the bread and butter. “And air you a-going to Joseph?”

50
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:30,796
“In heaven’s name,” said I, firing in spite of myself,

51
00:04:30,796 --> 00:04:35,078
“what does it matter to you where I am going? Leave that teapot alone.”

52
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:37,319
It was the worst course I could have taken,

53
00:04:37,319 --> 00:04:40,718
because it gave Pumblechook the opportunity he wanted.

54
00:04:40,718 --> 00:04:45,160
“Yes, young man,” said he, releasing the handle of the article in question,

55
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:49,277
retiring a step or two from my table, and speaking for the behoof of the

56
00:04:49,277 --> 00:04:53,879
landlord and waiter at the door, “I will leave that teapot alone. You are right,

57
00:04:53,879 --> 00:04:59,718
young man. For once you are right. I forgit myself when I take such an interest in your breakfast,

58
00:04:59,718 --> 00:05:05,160
as to wish your frame, exhausted by the debilitating effects of prodigygality,

59
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:12,116
to be stimilated by the ’olesome nourishment of your forefathers. And yet,” said Pumblechook,

60
00:05:12,116 --> 00:05:17,796
turning to the landlord and waiter, and pointing me out at arm’s length, “this is him as I ever

61
00:05:17,796 --> 00:05:24,957
sported with in his days of happy infancy! Tell me not it cannot be; I tell you this is him!”

62
00:05:24,957 --> 00:05:32,277
A low murmur from the two replied. The waiter appeared to be particularly affected.

63
00:05:32,277 --> 00:05:36,838
“This is him,” said Pumblechook, “as I have rode in my shay-cart.

64
00:05:37,637 --> 00:05:42,796
This is him as I have seen brought up by hand. This is him untoe the sister of which I was

65
00:05:42,796 --> 00:05:51,319
uncle by marriage, as her name was Georgiana M’ria from her own mother, let him deny it if he can!”

66
00:05:51,319 --> 00:05:57,557
The waiter seemed convinced that I could not deny it, and that it gave the case a black look.

67
00:05:57,559 --> 00:06:02,438
“Young man,” said Pumblechook, screwing his head at me in the old fashion,

68
00:06:02,438 --> 00:06:05,438
“you air a-going to Joseph. What does it matter to me,

69
00:06:05,438 --> 00:06:11,476
you ask me, where you air a-going? I say to you, Sir, you air a-going to Joseph.”

70
00:06:11,476 --> 00:06:15,879
The waiter coughed, as if he modestly invited me to get over that.

71
00:06:15,879 --> 00:06:21,476
“Now,” said Pumblechook, and all this with a most exasperating air of saying in the cause

72
00:06:21,476 --> 00:06:26,758
of virtue what was perfectly convincing and conclusive, “I will tell you what to

73
00:06:26,758 --> 00:06:33,038
say to Joseph. Here is Squires of the Boar present, known and respected in this town,

74
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:39,078
and here is William, which his father’s name was Potkins if I do not deceive myself.”

75
00:06:39,078 --> 00:06:41,637
“You do not, sir,” said William.

76
00:06:41,637 --> 00:06:49,277
“In their presence,” pursued Pumblechook, “I will tell you, young man, what to say to Joseph. Says

77
00:06:49,277 --> 00:06:56,116
you, “Joseph, I have this day seen my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortun’s. I will

78
00:06:56,116 --> 00:07:02,519
name no names, Joseph, but so they are pleased to call him up town, and I have seen that man.”

79
00:07:02,519 --> 00:07:05,319
“I swear I don’t see him here,” said I.

80
00:07:05,319 --> 00:07:09,436
“Say that likewise,” retorted Pumblechook. “Say you said that,

81
00:07:09,438 --> 00:07:12,917
and even Joseph will probably betray surprise.”

82
00:07:12,917 --> 00:07:17,116
“There you quite mistake him,” said I. “I know better.”

83
00:07:17,116 --> 00:07:21,436
“Says you,” Pumblechook went on, “‘Joseph, I have seen that man,

84
00:07:21,438 --> 00:07:26,796
and that man bears you no malice and bears me no malice. He knows your character, Joseph,

85
00:07:26,796 --> 00:07:31,597
and is well acquainted with your pig-headedness and ignorance; and he knows my character, Joseph,

86
00:07:31,597 --> 00:07:38,438
and he knows my want of gratitoode. Yes, Joseph,’ says you,” here Pumblechook shook his head and

87
00:07:38,438 --> 00:07:44,199
hand at me, “‘he knows my total deficiency of common human gratitoode. He knows it,

88
00:07:44,199 --> 00:07:51,436
Joseph, as none can. You do not know it, Joseph, having no call to know it, but that man do.’”

89
00:07:51,438 --> 00:07:57,680
Windy donkey as he was, it really amazed me that he could have the face to talk thus to mine.

90
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:00,839
“Says you, ‘Joseph, he gave me a little message,

91
00:08:00,839 --> 00:08:06,637
which I will now repeat. It was that, in my being brought low, he saw the finger of Providence.

92
00:08:07,796 --> 00:08:13,758
He knowed that finger when he saw Joseph, and he saw it plain. It pinted out this writing,

93
00:08:13,758 --> 00:08:21,879
Joseph. Reward of ingratitoode to his earliest benefactor, and founder of fortun’s. But that man

94
00:08:21,879 --> 00:08:27,438
said he did not repent of what he had done, Joseph. Not at all. It was right to do it,

95
00:08:27,438 --> 00:08:33,758
it was kind to do it, it was benevolent to do it, and he would do it again.’”

96
00:08:33,758 --> 00:08:38,358
“It’s pity,” said I, scornfully, as I finished my interrupted breakfast,

97
00:08:38,358 --> 00:08:43,319
“that the man did not say what he had done and would do again.”

98
00:08:43,319 --> 00:08:50,718
“Squires of the Boar!” Pumblechook was now addressing the landlord, “and William! I have

99
00:08:50,718 --> 00:08:56,519
no objections to your mentioning, either up town or down town, if such should be your wishes, that

100
00:08:56,519 --> 00:09:04,637
it was right to do it, kind to do it, benevolent to do it, and that I would do it again.”

101
00:09:04,637 --> 00:09:11,597
With those words the Impostor shook them both by the hand, with an air, and left the house;

102
00:09:11,597 --> 00:09:18,519
leaving me much more astonished than delighted by the virtues of that same indefinite “it.” I

103
00:09:18,519 --> 00:09:22,839
was not long after him in leaving the house too, and when I went down the High Street I

104
00:09:22,839 --> 00:09:29,078
saw him holding forth (no doubt to the same effect) at his shop door to a select group,

105
00:09:29,078 --> 00:09:35,758
who honoured me with very unfavourable glances as I passed on the opposite side of the way.

106
00:09:35,758 --> 00:09:41,038
But, it was only the pleasanter to turn to Biddy and to Joe, whose great forbearance shone more

107
00:09:41,038 --> 00:09:48,637
brightly than before, if that could be, contrasted with this brazen pretender. I went towards them

108
00:09:48,637 --> 00:09:55,758
slowly, for my limbs were weak, but with a sense of increasing relief as I drew nearer to them,

109
00:09:55,758 --> 00:10:01,879
and a sense of leaving arrogance and untruthfulness further and further behind.

110
00:10:01,879 --> 00:10:08,680
The June weather was delicious. The sky was blue, the larks were soaring high over the green corn,

111
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:13,277
I thought all that countryside more beautiful and peaceful by far than I had ever known it to

112
00:10:13,277 --> 00:10:20,000
be yet. Many pleasant pictures of the life that I would lead there, and of the change for the better

113
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,358
that would come over my character when I had a guiding spirit at my side whose simple faith

114
00:10:25,358 --> 00:10:33,557
and clear home wisdom I had proved, beguiled my way. They awakened a tender emotion in me;

115
00:10:33,559 --> 00:10:40,116
for my heart was softened by my return, and such a change had come to pass, that I felt like one who

116
00:10:40,116 --> 00:10:46,839
was toiling home barefoot from distant travel, and whose wanderings had lasted many years.

117
00:10:46,839 --> 00:10:52,796
The schoolhouse where Biddy was mistress I had never seen; but, the little roundabout lane by

118
00:10:52,796 --> 00:10:58,957
which I entered the village, for quietness’ sake, took me past it. I was disappointed to

119
00:10:58,957 --> 00:11:06,559
find that the day was a holiday; no children were there, and Biddy’s house was closed. Some hopeful

120
00:11:06,559 --> 00:11:11,519
notion of seeing her, busily engaged in her daily duties, before she saw me,

121
00:11:11,519 --> 00:11:18,796
had been in my mind and was defeated. But the forge was a very short distance off,

122
00:11:18,796 --> 00:11:25,237
and I went towards it under the sweet green limes, listening for the clink of Joe’s hammer. Long

123
00:11:25,237 --> 00:11:31,476
after I ought to have heard it, and long after I had fancied I heard it and found it but a fancy,

124
00:11:31,476 --> 00:11:38,078
all was still. The limes were there, and the white thorns were there, and the chestnut-trees

125
00:11:38,078 --> 00:11:44,277
were there, and their leaves rustled harmoniously when I stopped to listen; but, the clink of Joe’s

126
00:11:44,277 --> 00:11:49,756
hammer was not in the midsummer wind. Almost fearing, without knowing why,

127
00:11:49,758 --> 00:11:57,237
to come in view of the forge, I saw it at last, and saw that it was closed. No gleam of fire,

128
00:11:57,237 --> 00:12:04,436
no glittering shower of sparks, no roar of bellows; all shut up, and still.

129
00:12:04,438 --> 00:12:09,680
But the house was not deserted, and the best parlour seemed to be in use, for there were white

130
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:16,398
curtains fluttering in its window, and the window was open and gay with flowers. I went softly

131
00:12:16,398 --> 00:12:22,796
towards it, meaning to peep over the flowers, when Joe and Biddy stood before me, arm in arm.

132
00:12:22,796 --> 00:12:27,116
At first Biddy gave a cry, as if she thought it was my apparition,

133
00:12:27,116 --> 00:12:34,879
but in another moment she was in my embrace. I wept to see her, and she wept to see me; I,

134
00:12:34,879 --> 00:12:40,398
because she looked so fresh and pleasant; she, because I looked so worn and white.

135
00:12:40,398 --> 00:12:43,398
“But dear Biddy, how smart you are!”

136
00:12:43,398 --> 00:12:44,917
“Yes, dear Pip.”

137
00:12:44,917 --> 00:12:47,277
“And Joe, how smart you are!”

138
00:12:47,277 --> 00:12:50,038
“Yes, dear old Pip, old chap.”

139
00:12:50,038 --> 00:12:53,839
I looked at both of them, from one to the other, and then—

140
00:12:53,839 --> 00:13:00,078
“It’s my wedding-day!” cried Biddy, in a burst of happiness, “and I am married to Joe!”

141
00:13:00,078 --> 00:13:05,758
They had taken me into the kitchen, and I had laid my head down on the old deal table. Biddy

142
00:13:05,758 --> 00:13:11,839
held one of my hands to her lips, and Joe’s restoring touch was on my shoulder. “Which

143
00:13:11,839 --> 00:13:17,637
he warn’t strong enough, my dear, fur to be surprised,” said Joe. And Biddy said,

144
00:13:17,637 --> 00:13:22,637
“I ought to have thought of it, dear Joe, but I was too happy.” They were

145
00:13:22,637 --> 00:13:28,438
both so overjoyed to see me, so proud to see me, so touched by my coming to them,

146
00:13:28,438 --> 00:13:33,917
so delighted that I should have come by accident to make their day complete!

147
00:13:33,917 --> 00:13:38,879
My first thought was one of great thankfulness that I had never breathed this last baffled

148
00:13:38,879 --> 00:13:45,839
hope to Joe. How often, while he was with me in my illness, had it risen to my lips!

149
00:13:45,839 --> 00:13:52,597
How irrevocable would have been his knowledge of it, if he had remained with me but another hour!

150
00:13:52,597 --> 00:13:57,879
“Dear Biddy,” said I, “you have the best husband in the whole world, and if you

151
00:13:57,879 --> 00:14:05,680
could have seen him by my bed you would have—But no, you couldn’t love him better than you do.”

152
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:08,160
“No, I couldn’t indeed,” said Biddy.

153
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:12,358
“And, dear Joe, you have the best wife in the whole world,

154
00:14:12,358 --> 00:14:19,199
and she will make you as happy as even you deserve to be, you dear, good, noble Joe!”

155
00:14:19,199 --> 00:14:25,116
Joe looked at me with a quivering lip, and fairly put his sleeve before his eyes.

156
00:14:25,116 --> 00:14:30,637
“And Joe and Biddy both, as you have been to church to-day, and are in charity and love

157
00:14:30,637 --> 00:14:37,199
with all mankind, receive my humble thanks for all you have done for me, and all I have so ill

158
00:14:37,199 --> 00:14:44,718
repaid! And when I say that I am going away within the hour, for I am soon going abroad, and that

159
00:14:44,718 --> 00:14:49,476
I shall never rest until I have worked for the money with which you have kept me out of prison,

160
00:14:49,476 --> 00:14:55,796
and have sent it to you, don’t think, dear Joe and Biddy, that if I could repay it a thousand times

161
00:14:55,796 --> 00:15:03,000
over, I suppose I could cancel a farthing of the debt I owe you, or that I would do so if I could!”

162
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:08,559
They were both melted by these words, and both entreated me to say no more.

163
00:15:08,559 --> 00:15:14,839
“But I must say more. Dear Joe, I hope you will have children to love,

164
00:15:14,839 --> 00:15:18,917
and that some little fellow will sit in this chimney-corner of a winter night,

165
00:15:18,917 --> 00:15:24,398
who may remind you of another little fellow gone out of it for ever. Don’t tell him,

166
00:15:24,398 --> 00:15:30,678
Joe, that I was thankless; don’t tell him, Biddy, that I was ungenerous and unjust;

167
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:36,597
only tell him that I honoured you both, because you were both so good and true, and that,

168
00:15:36,597 --> 00:15:42,319
as your child, I said it would be natural to him to grow up a much better man than I did.”

169
00:15:42,319 --> 00:15:46,635
“I ain’t a-going,” said Joe, from behind his sleeve, “to tell him nothink o’ that natur,

170
00:15:46,637 --> 00:15:50,398
Pip. Nor Biddy ain’t. Nor yet no one ain’t.”

171
00:15:50,398 --> 00:15:55,277
“And now, though I know you have already done it in your own kind hearts, pray tell me,

172
00:15:55,277 --> 00:15:59,237
both, that you forgive me! Pray let me hear you say the words,

173
00:15:59,237 --> 00:16:02,718
that I may carry the sound of them away with me, and then I shall be

174
00:16:02,718 --> 00:16:08,758
able to believe that you can trust me, and think better of me, in the time to come!”

175
00:16:08,758 --> 00:16:16,398
“O dear old Pip, old chap,” said Joe. “God knows as I forgive you, if I have anythink to forgive!”

176
00:16:16,398 --> 00:16:21,116
“Amen! And God knows I do!” echoed Biddy.

177
00:16:21,116 --> 00:16:23,718
“Now let me go up and look at my old little room,

178
00:16:23,718 --> 00:16:29,160
and rest there a few minutes by myself. And then, when I have eaten and drunk with you,

179
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:35,475
go with me as far as the finger-post, dear Joe and Biddy, before we say good-bye!”

180
00:16:35,476 --> 00:16:41,241
I sold all I had, and put aside as much as I could, for a composition with my creditors,—who

181
00:16:41,241 --> 00:16:48,358
gave me ample time to pay them in full,—and I went out and joined Herbert. Within a month, I had

182
00:16:48,358 --> 00:16:54,922
quitted England, and within two months I was clerk to Clarriker and Co., and within four months I

183
00:16:54,922 --> 00:17:02,000
assumed my first undivided responsibility. For the beam across the parlour ceiling at Mill Pond Bank

184
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:08,320
had then ceased to tremble under old Bill Barley’s growls and was at peace, and Herbert had gone away

185
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:15,600
to marry Clara, and I was left in sole charge of the Eastern Branch until he brought her back.

186
00:17:15,601 --> 00:17:18,960
Many a year went round before I was a partner in the House;

187
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:23,758
but I lived happily with Herbert and his wife, and lived frugally, and paid my debts, and maintained

188
00:17:23,758 --> 00:17:29,679
a constant correspondence with Biddy and Joe. It was not until I became third in the Firm,

189
00:17:29,679 --> 00:17:36,476
that Clarriker betrayed me to Herbert; but he then declared that the secret of Herbert’s partnership

190
00:17:36,476 --> 00:17:42,398
had been long enough upon his conscience, and he must tell it. So he told it,

191
00:17:42,398 --> 00:17:47,835
and Herbert was as much moved as amazed, and the dear fellow and I were not the worse friends for

192
00:17:47,835 --> 00:17:54,358
the long concealment. I must not leave it to be supposed that we were ever a great House,

193
00:17:54,358 --> 00:18:01,039
or that we made mints of money. We were not in a grand way of business, but we had a good name,

194
00:18:01,039 --> 00:18:06,437
and worked for our profits, and did very well. We owed so much to Herbert’s ever cheerful

195
00:18:06,437 --> 00:18:12,554
industry and readiness, that I often wondered how I had conceived that old idea of his inaptitude,

196
00:18:12,554 --> 00:18:17,758
until I was one day enlightened by the reflection, that perhaps the inaptitude had never been in him

197
00:18:17,758 --> 00:18:21,000
at all, but had been in me. Chapter 59.
